# Docker Load Scaling

## Overview

The app runs **multiple frontend and backend containers** in production when using the scale override. Same Postgres, Redis, and uploads volume; only more API/Next workers.

## Repo pieces

- **apps/backend/docker-compose.scale.yml** – Adds `strapi-2` (1338) through `strapi-6` (1342). Use with `docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.scale.yml`.
- **apps/backend/docker-compose.ci.yml** – GitLab CI only: overrides main `strapi` image to registry.cyandm.org.
- **apps/backend/docker-compose.scale.ci.yml** – GitLab CI only: overrides scale service images (strapi-2…strapi-6) to registry.cyandm.org.
- **apps/frontend/docker-compose.scale.yml** – Adds `frontend-2` (3001) through `frontend-12` (3011). Same image/env.
- **apps/frontend/docker-compose.ci.yml** – GitLab CI only: overrides main `frontend` image to registry.cyandm.org.
- **apps/frontend/docker-compose.scale.ci.yml** – GitLab CI only: overrides scale service images (frontend-2…frontend-12) to registry.cyandm.org.
- **GitLab CI** – `.gitlab-ci.yml` deploy uses compose order: `-f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.ci.yml -f docker-compose.scale.yml -f docker-compose.scale.ci.yml` (base → base CI override → scale → scale CI override). Copies all four files.
- **GitHub Actions** – `frontend-cicd.yml` and `backend-cicd.yml` use base + scale only (no CI override); images from ghcr.io.
- **Nginx** – Server config must use upstreams for load balancing. Snippet: **docs/nginx-upstream-snippet.conf** (include before `server {}`, then `proxy_pass http://next_upstream` and `proxy_pass http://strapi_upstream`). Full steps: **docs/DOCKER-LOAD-SCALING-GUIDE.md**.

## Conventions

- Backend scale: five extra Strapi (`strapi-2`…`strapi-6`), same volumes/networks as base (6 Strapi total).
- Frontend scale: eleven extra Next.js services (`frontend-2`–`frontend-12`), same env_file (12 Next total).
- Strapi upstream uses `ip_hash` so admin sessions stick to one instance. Next upstream uses `ip_hash` so the same client tends to hit the same Next.js instance and per-instance L1 cache is effective (avoids "slow then fast then slow" with Redis cache handler).
- To run single-instance on server: omit the scale file (e.g. `docker compose up -d` only).
